ey generate (LTV), the business model is unsustainable. So, here's how I'll frame my response: I'll start by validating the user's observation directly, explaining that it's a very real and common problem, often referred to as "app abandonment" or "app rot." Then, I'll delve into the specifics: * **The Harsh Reality of Free Apps:** * To make minimum wage from banner ads, you'd need *hundreds of thousands* of active users. Most free apps get a few hundred downloads, maybe a few thousand if they are lucky. eCPM (effective cost per thousand impressions) is generally quite low. * **Keeping an App Alive Isn't Free:** * Apps aren't "set it and forget it." Apple and Google constantly update iOS and Android. If a developer doesn't update their code, the app breaks or gets delisted. There are also server costs (AWS, database) that scale up with user adoption. * **The "Hobbyist" Factor is Significant:** * Many indie apps are built by students, people learning to code, or developers building a portfolio. Once they get the job or lose interest, the app is often abandoned. * **User Acquisition is Expensive:** * The app stores are insanely crowded. To get noticed, developers have to pay for marketing (ads, promotions, etc.). If marketing costs more than the app generates (CAC > LTV), it's a failing business. Finally, I'll conclude by summarizing the reality, emphasizing that the success stories are the exception, not the rule. The vast majority of free apps are passion projects that eventually hit economic realities. That's why the freemium and subscription models have become so prevalent; they offer the only pathway to real sustainability.
14:53:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You have hit the nail on the head. What you are noticing is a very real, widely recognized phenomenon in the tech world often referred to as **"app abandonment"** or **"app rot."** Your intuition is 100% correct: the vast majority of independent creators do not make nearly enough money to justify the time, effort, and financial cost of keeping a free app updated. Here is the harsh reality behind why so many free apps die within a year: ### 1. The Brutal Math of Ad Revenue In my previous answer, I mentioned advertising, but I didn't mention the scale required to make a living from it. Ad networks pay fractions of a penny per view. * To make just $10 a day from banner ads, an app might need 5,000 to 10,000 active users opening the app every single day. * Most independent apps are lucky to get a few hundred downloads *total*. When a developer realizes they spent 300 hours building an app only to earn $2.14 a month in ad revenue, they usually abandon it. ### 2. Apps Are Expensive to Keep Alive People often think that once an app is built, the work is done. In reality, software requires constant, unpaid maintenance: * **OS Updates:** Apple and Google update iOS and Android every year. If a developer doesn't update their app's code to match the new operating systems, the app will start crashing, and the app stores will eventually hide or delete it. * **Server Costs:** If the app uses the internet at all (to store user accounts, save high scores, or host images), the developer has to pay monthly server fees. If an app gets popular but doesn't monetize well, the developer can actually *lose* money paying for servers. * **Developer Fees:** Apple charges developers $99 every single year just to keep their apps listed on the App Store. If the app isn't making $99 a year, the developer will just let their account expire, and the app vanishes. ### 3. The "Portfolio Project" Reality Millions of free apps are not created by businesses; they are created by hobbyists, college students, or junior developers trying to build a portfolio. They make a free app to prove they know how to code so they can get hired by a big tech company. Once they land a high-paying job, they no longer have the time or need to maintain their free app, so it dies. ### 4. The App Store is a "Winner-Takes-All" Market There are over 5 million apps between the Apple App Store and Google Play Store. The top 1% of apps (made by massive companies like Meta, Google, and giant gaming studios) make 99% of the money. They have millions of dollars to spend on marketing and user acquisition. An independent developer simply cannot compete for your attention, and without attention, they can't make money. ### 5. Customer Support is Exhausting Even if an app is completely free, users will leave 1-star reviews if there is a minor bug, or they will flood the developer’s inbox demanding new features. For a solo developer making no money, the psychological toll of dealing with angry users for a free product is a massive reason they just walk away. **The Takeaway** You are witnessing the exact reason why the app industry has aggressively shifted toward **subscriptions**. Developers realized that one-time purchases and tiny ad revenues weren't enough to survive. To justify the endless cycle of updates, server costs, and bug fixes, they *have* to charge users $3 or $5 a month. So, when you see a free app get abandoned, you are entirely right: it was likely a passion project that finally collided with the harsh economics of software development.
